### Git Projects

Milestone vs label. 
A label is a tag for categorising by kind (bug, documentation). 
A milestone is for grouping by goal, and it tracks completion. Labels don't have dates or progress.

Milestone vs iteration. 
A milestone lives on the repository, is one-off, and has a single due date. 
An iteration lives in a project as a custom field and repeats on a cadence. 
Sprints → iteration. 
A release with a deadline → milestone.

Milestone vs release. 
A release is a published, downloadable package tied to a Git tag. 
A milestone is planning: the work you intend to finish. 
You might use a milestone to track the work, then publish a release when it's done.

Scope. 
A milestone only holds items from its own repository. 
A project can span many repositories, which is why teams working across repos plan in Projects and use milestones per repo.

###Notes:
Project — a planning board that pulls issues and PRs from any number of repos into one place.
Table / Board / Roadmap — the three ways to look at a project: spreadsheet, kanban columns, timeline.
Custom field — an extra column you define on a project (text, number, date, single select, iteration).
Single select — a field with a fixed list of choices, so you can group and filter reliably.
Iteration — a repeating time box (sprints) that generates dated periods automatically.
Milestone — a repository goal with an optional due date and a percent-complete bar.
Label — a tag for what kind of thing an issue is, like bug or documentation.
Assignee — who is responsible for an issue or PR, up to 10 people.
Release — a published, downloadable package tied to a Git tag.
